7. An application under section 30 of the Act for setting aside the Award had been laid in the Court on 16-2-87. Taking the grounds that, (i) that no notice of Arbitration had been served upon the respondent and the award was liable to be set aside on account of the misconduct on the part of the Arbitrator, (ii) there had been no appointment of Arbitrator as per provision of Arbitration Agreement and the Award was bad, (iii) The Arbitrator arrived at an erroneous decision in giving award in favour of the appellant and, (iv) Arbitrator acted beyond his jurisdiction by assuming power which he did not possess and thus misconducted himself. The application under section 5 of the Limitation Act was also laid on 17-2-87 for condo nation of delay in presenting the said application. Another application in the term of objection under section 33 of the Act had been, also, presented before the Court on 30-9-87. The grounds taken in the application under section 30 of the Act had been taken as grounds in the application under section 33 of the Act.&nbsp; 

 8. The learned Subordinate Judge in deciding the application under section 14(2) of the Act took the view that the second party (respondent) did not show any proof of Neutron Radiation of the monkeys which were sold to the Armed Forced Radiobiology Research Institute and then rendered decision that the breach of the contract by selling 6 monkeys from 5000 monkeys is a material breach warranting an opportunity to the party to terminate the contract and the Arbitrator committed misconduct. Further view taken was that the selection of the sole-Arbitrator was beyond the purview of the contractual right and the Award given by the sole-Arbitrator was illegal and void. With the above view, the learned Judge refused to make the Award Rule of the Court and set aside the Award.&nbsp; 

 (iv) The learned Subordinate Judge committed an error of law in setting aside the Award in the absence of misconduct on the part of the learned Arbitrator and no such objection having been taken within the statutory period and no deposit having been made as provided in section 33 of the Arbitration Act, the order setting aside the Award is illegal. 

 (v) Article 33 of the Agreement provided that the monkeys exported would be exclusively used for the purpose of medical and other scientific research by highly skilled and competent persons for the general benefit of all the people of the world and the appellant did not violate the term of the Agreement by selling 6(six) monkeys to the Armed Forces Research Institute for scientific research and the learned Subordinate Judge committed error of law in holding that the Arbitrator committed misconduct in this regard which cannot be termed as misconduct within the meaning of section 30 of the Arbitration Act.&nbsp; 

 14. Section 9 gives power to appoint new Arbitrator or, in certain cases, a sole Arbitrator. Section 9(b) reads thus:&nbsp; 

 (a) that an arbitrator or umpire has misconducted himself or the proceedings;&nbsp; 

 19. Clause (a) provides that an award shall be set aside where an arbitrator has misconducted himself in the proceeding. In Black&#39;&#39;s Law Dictionary, Sixth Edition &quot;Misconduct&quot; is stated thus:&nbsp; 

 In Corpus Juris Secundum Vol. 58 page 818 &quot;Misconduct&quot; is stated hereunder:&nbsp; 

 &quot;Both in law and in ordinary speech the term &quot;misconduct&quot; usually implies an act done wilfully with a wrong intention, and conveys the idea of intentional wrongdoing. The term implies fault beyond the error of judgment; a wrongful intention, and not a mere error of judgment; but it does not necessarily imply corruption or criminal intention, and, in the legal idea of misconduct, an evil intention is not a necessary ingredient.&quot;&nbsp; 

 &quot;the expression &#39;&#39;misconducted the proceedings&#39;&#39; does not really amount to much more than such a mis-handling of the arbitration as is likely to cause some substantial miscarriage of justice, and one instance that may be given is where the arbitrator refuses to hear evidence upon a material issue.&quot;&nbsp; 

 &quot;the expression &#39;&#39;misconduct&#39;&#39; is &quot;of wide import&quot; and includes : &quot;on the one hand bribery and corruption and on the other hand a mere mistake as to the scope of authority conferred by the agreement of reference or an error of law appearing on the face of the award. Thus misconduct occurs if the arbitrator or umpire, as the case may be, fails to decide all the matters which are referred to him; if by his award he purports to decide matters which have not, in fact, been included in the agreement of reference; if the award is inconsistent.&quot;&nbsp; 

 20. Clause (c) makes the invalidity of the award as a ground for setting it aside. The expression &quot;when the award is otherwise invalid&quot; has been the subject matter of judicial consideration in a number of cases. Ram Protap Chamira vs Durga Prasad Chamira and others AIR 1925 Privy Council 293 may be profitably noticed. In this regard the Judicial Committee of the Privy Council stated thus: &quot;An award made otherwise than in accordance with the authority conferred on the arbitrator is an award which is otherwise &quot;invalid&quot; and may accordingly be set aside by the Court.&quot;&nbsp; 

 23. This brings us to the consideration of the pertinent question, whether the Arbitrator in awarding the Award has misconducted the proceeding and the Award suffered from invalidity and, also, whether the learned Subordinate Judge was well founded in setting aside the Award on the question of misconduct and invalidity.&nbsp; 

 25. The appellant appointed Mr Jacob B Tanzer, the former Justice of the Supreme Court of the State of Oregon, as Arbitrator on 9-3-1984 and on the same date issued notice upon the respondent (Lieutenant General Hussain Mohammad Ershad, Chief Martial Law Administrator Bangladesh, Dhaka and Ministry of Agriculture, Government of the People&#39;&#39;s Republic of Bangladesh), Dhaka intimating that the appellant (MOL Enterprise Inc) designated as Arbitrator the Hon&#39;&#39;ble Jacob B Tanzer, former Justice of the Supreme Court of the State of Oregon and the respondent was requested to advise as to its appointment of the Arbitrator. The respondent having not appointed its Arbitrator, the appellant appointed sole arbitrator on 5-3-1985. Award was given on 3-4-1985. Application under section 14(2) of the Act for making the Award Rule of the Court was made on 8-5-1986. Notice upon the respondent was served on 18-2-1986. The application under section 30 for setting aside the Award was laid on 16-2-1987 which is after a period of 30 days as enshrined in Article 158 of the Limitation Act. The application under section 5 of the Limitation Act for condo nation of delay was filed on 17-2-1987. The application stood rejected by the Court on 18-9-1988. The application under section 33 of the Act for setting aside the award was filed on 30-9-1987 long after the expiry of 30 days&#39;&#39; limitation. The said application was not even supported by an affidavit as enjoined in the section itself. Again, the same was not accompanied either by deposit of the award money or furnishing security for the same. No notice, thus, can be taken of the said application under section 33 of the Act. It can be suggested that there was no case laid from the side of the respondent before the Court for setting aside the Award inasmuch as it is only when the application is formulated and placed before the Court in accordance with law that the party may be regarded as having applied to the Court for setting aside the Award. The application under section 30 having been stood rejected by the Court, there was no scope left for the Court to take into consideration the contentions/pleas advanced from the respondent. The application challenging the validity of the Award having been filed beyond limitation, the same could not be entertained and section 5 of the Limitation Act was not at all applicable for condo nation of delay. Similarly, the Court also got no jurisdiction to decide the proceeding under section 14 of the Act. Support for this proposition of law is sought to be drawn from authorities placed before us on behalf of the appellant which are Mohamed Esoof vs VR Subramanyam and another AIR, 1957 Mysore 78, Ganesh Chandra Misra vs Artatrana Misra, and others, AIR 1965 Orissa 17, Hastimal Dalichand Bora and others, AIR 1965 Orissa 17, Hastimal Balichand Bora and others vs Hiralal Motichand Mutha, AIR 1954 Bombay 243, Divi Dilla vs Babu Ram, AIR 1927 Lahore 273, wherein it is laid down that a Court has no jurisdiction to entertain any application for setting aside an award if it is filed after the period of limitation of 30 days as enshrined in Article 158 of the Limitation Act and the Court also got no jurisdiction to condone the delay under section 5 of the Limitation Act which got no manner of application in this regard. In the unreported case Civil Appeal No. 1 of 1984 Khurshid Anwar and another appellants vs Jamil Akhtar Respondent arising out of Civil Revision No. 149(c) of 1982, Judgment dated January 14, 1985 their Lordships of the Appellate Division considered the question whether the inherent power under section 151 of the Code of Civil Procedure was attracted in condoning the delay in entertaining an objection to the Award given by the Arbitrator which was filed beyond 30 days as prescribed in Article 158 of the Limitation Act. The Appellate Division answered the question in the negative. The observations of the Appellate Division is couched in the following language:&nbsp; 

 &quot;This clearly shows that the respondent got knowledge of the filing of the award in Court and, as such, even if limitation starts from this date still the objection is found to have been filed beyond 30 days as prescribed in Article 158 of the Limitation Act. The provision for exercising Court&#39;&#39;s inherent power under section 151 of the Civil Procedure Code is not attracted to a case specifically governed by statute&quot;.&nbsp; 

 26. True it is that the Court got the power to set aside an award suo motu if ground exists in exercising that power. An award can be set aside where the same is a nullity on the face of the award. The decision rendered by the Arbitrator that the breach of the contract by selling 6 monkeys out of 5000 monkeys would not be material breach warranting termination of the contract was not accepted by the Court and it recorded the finding that the Arbitrator committed misconduct in that regard. The controversy whether the Arbitrator misconducted himself or the proceeding itself needs resolution now.&nbsp; 

 27. The term &quot;misconduct&quot; has been stated in the foregoing paragraphs. The word &quot;misconduct&quot; means conduct which reflects upon the integrity and impartiality of the Arbitrator. It also means conduct amounting to moral turpitude, breach and neglect of duty, failure to perform essential duties which are couched on the Arbitrator mishandling of the Arbitration proceeding amounting to some substantial displacement of the ordinary rules of justice. Neglect of duty on the part of the Arbitrator which is likely to lead to substantial miscarriage of justice and gross negligence or recklessness on the face of the award. The arbitrator who Acts within the scope of his powers and duties in making the award the same cannot be challenged on the ground of &quot;misconduct&quot;.&nbsp; 

 28. Under the Agreement the breeding farm was to export 5000 monkeys during the first year, the Agreement being for a period of 10 years. The Arbitrator was of the positive view that selling of 6 monkeys out of 5000 monkeys, if a breach of clause 33 of the Agreement, would not be a material breach leading to termination of the agreement. This decision rendered by the Arbitrator cannot be characterized as a &quot;misconduct&quot; and it cannot be said that the Arbitrator misconducted himself in making the award. On the part of the Arbitrator there is no corruption nor conduct amounting to moral turpitude, no breach and neglect of duty and responsibility causing miscarriage of justice, no failure to perform the essential duties, no mishandling of the Arbitration proceeding amounting to some substantial displacement of the ordinary rules of justice and no indication of gross negligence or recklessness on the face of the award. The decision recorded by the Court on the point of &quot;misconduct&quot; does not appear to be tenable.&nbsp;&nbsp; 

 33. It has already been stated that the Court in disposing of an application under section 14(2) of the Act cannot sit on appeal on the decisions rendered by the Arbitrator. An Arbitrator is a Judge of all matters whether of fact or of law and a Court hearing objection against an award is not a Court of appeal sitting in judgment over the award. A Court while deciding whether a certain award should be made a rule of the Court is not sitting as a Court of appeal. This proposition of law has been well settled by judicial pronouncement of the Apex Courts of the sub-continent. S Sarabjit vs State of Punjab and another, AIR 1985 Punjab and Hariyana 179 and Puri Construction Pvt Ltd vs Union of India AIR 1989 (SC) 777 relied upon from the side of the appellant may be noticed here. In S Sarabjit Singh case it is stated:&nbsp; 

 &quot;Court cannot sit in appeal over conclusions of arbitrator by reexamining and re-appraising evidence considered by Arbitrator. In Puri Construction Pvt Ltd case the observation is couched in the following language:&nbsp; 

 35. We now pass on the authorities relied upon by the respondent in support of the contentions raised. Before adverting to the authorities it is to be kept in view that each and every judgment must be read in the facts and circumstances of each and every case and there cannot be any general application of any proposition of law settled in any case.&nbsp; 

 36. It is also needed to be stated that we shall not make an ideal parade of discussing all the authorities cited but only consider those which got some application or relevancy in the facts and circumstances of the case in our hand. In Abdul Khaleq vs Province of East Pakistan and another, 15 DLR 283 two principles has been enunciated, (i) where a party appoints his own Arbitrator to act as the sole Arbitrator without serving any notice contemplated under section 9(b) of the Act to the other party, the proceeding taken by the sole Arbitrator and the Award made by him are without jurisdiction and void, (ii) Objection as to the validity of the award is to be taken within 30 days as emphasized under Article 158 of the Limitation Act. The propositions of law so settled in the said case are well recognized and there cannot be any dispute. But the first proposition of law is not at all applicable in the facts and circumstances of the case in hand inasmuch as the sole Arbitrator was appointed with a service of notice upon the respondent as contemplated under section 9(b) of the Act. The second proposition absolutely goes against the respondent. In Messers R Sim and Company, Narayanganj vs Messers Pak Industries (jute Mills) Ltd, Tanbazar, Narayanganj 8 DLR 305 it was held that when an award is illegal and void for want of jurisdiction apparent on the face of it, it is not necessary that the parties should file objection to the award within the prescribed period under Article 158 of the Limitation Act. In Messrs Badri Narayan Agarwalla vs Messrs Pak Jute Balers Ltd., Dacca PLD 1966 (Dacca) 262 it is observed that provision of Article 158 of the Limitation Act is not attracted when the award is void ab initio and the party is entitled to put forward objection to such award even beyond period of limitation provided by Article 158 of the Limitation Act. In the case in hand we hold the view that the Award passed by the Arbitrator is not void and, as such, the proposition of law decided in the case also got no manner of application and the respondent cannot have any assistance from this authority. In Bangladesh Water Development Board and others vs Progati Prakaushali and another 49 DLR 335 the principle enunciated is that the failure to make deposit as required by the proviso to section 33 of the Act renders the application under the section non-existent in the eye of law. The case goes against the respondent and it is not understood for what purpose the authority has been placed before us in support of the contention raised.&nbsp; 

 Having regard to the facts and circumstances of the case the parties are directed to bear their respective costs.&nbsp;
